window.RAD_DOCS = {
  "01_what_is_rad.md": "# Chapter 1: What is Rad?\n\n## 1.1 Philosophy & Design Goals\n\nThe **Rad programming language** is a modern, statically typed systems programming language designed for writing high-performance, predictable, and robust native software. Rad was created to offer the mechanical sympathy, raw performance, and low-level hardware control of C, paired with the safety, ergonomics, and clarity of modern languages\u2014without requiring a heavy runtime garbage collector or overly complex compile-time lifetime annotations.\n\n### Core Tenets\n\n1. **Self-Contained Static Executables**: Every binary produced by the Rad compiler (`radc`) is linked as a static Position-Independent Executable (`static-pie`) against a bundled Musl libc sysroot. Executables have zero dynamic library dependencies (`no PT_INTERP`, `no DT_NEEDED`), running reliably across any Linux kernel without distribution-specific runtime issues.\n2. **Zero-Overhead C & C++ Interoperability**: Rad speaks the C ABI natively. Standard C library functions (`malloc`, `free`, `puts`, `printf`, `system`, file I/O) are direct zero-cost calls without wrapper bloat, marshaling, or FFI barriers. Multi-language projects compile smoothly using bundled `rad-clang` and `rad-clang++`.\n3. **Predictable Memory Semantics**: Rad uses explicit value semantics, direct heap allocations via built-in dynamic collections (`String`, `Vec`), and transparent raw pointer manipulation when interfacing with hardware or legacy systems. There is no hidden runtime overhead, tracing garbage collection pauses, or hidden stop-the-world phases.\n4. **Self-Hosted, Single-Pass/Two-Pass Speed**: The production Rad compiler (`radc`) is written entirely in Rad. It compiles directly into SSA (Static Single Assignment) LLVM IR, pairing blazing compile times with LLVM's world-class optimization pipeline (`-O2`, `-O3`).\n5. **Batteries-Included Toolchain**: Rad ships as a unified toolchain comprising the compiler (`radc`), the package manager and build orchestrator (`rad`), the assembler/code generator (`rad-llc`), the linker (`rad-lld`), and full C/C++ compilers (`rad-clang`, `rad-clang++`).\n\n---\n\n## 1.2 Architectural Overview\n\nThe Rad ecosystem consists of three major layers working together:\n\n```\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502                 Rad Developer Applications                  \u2502\n\u2502             (src/*.rad, rad.toml, packages)                 \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                               \u2502\n                               \u25bc\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502         Rad Toolchain Orchestrator & Compiler Engine        \u2502\n\u2502   rad (Package Manager)       radc (Self-Hosted Compiler)   \u2502\n\u2502   - new, build, run, test     - Lexer & Pratt AST Parser    \u2502\n\u2502   - doctor, check, clean      - SSA LLVM IR Generator       \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                               \u2502\n                               \u25bc\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502             Bundled LLVM & Static Target Sysroot            \u2502\n\u2502   rad-llc (Code Generator)    rad-lld (Static Linker)       \u2502\n\u2502   Target: x86_64-unknown-linux-musl (rcrt1.o, libc.a)       \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                               \u2502\n                               \u25bc\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502              Standalone Native Executable                   \u2502\n\u2502           (Static PIE, Zero Shared Dependencies)            \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\n\n### The Component Pipeline\n\n- **`rad`**: The user-facing command-line interface. It manages project layout, creates packages (`rad new`), orchestrates builds (`rad build`), runs test suites (`rad test`), and checks system health (`rad doctor`).\n- **`radc`**: The self-hosted compiler. It tokenizes source files with a high-speed lexer, constructs an arena-based Abstract Syntax Tree using a Pratt recursive descent parser, and directly generates optimized textual LLVM IR (`.tmp.ll`).\n- **`rad-llc` & `rad-lld`**: Hermetic LLVM wrappers. `rad-llc` compiles LLVM IR into position-independent machine object files (`.o`), and `rad-lld` links them against Musl `rcrt1.o`, `libc.a`, and `libclang_rt.builtins` into a final static PIE binary.\n- **`rad-clang` & `rad-clang++`**: Integrated host C and C++ compilers configured with the same sysroot for zero-friction mixed-language software development.\n\n---\n\n## 1.3 How Rad Compares to Other Languages\n\n| Feature | Rad | C | C++ | Rust | Zig | Go |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Garbage Collection** | **None** | None | None | None | None | Concurrent Tracing GC |\n| **Runtime Overhead** | **Zero** | Zero | Minimal | Zero | Zero | Moderate (Scheduler, GC) |\n| **Default Binary Format** | **Static PIE** | Dynamic ELF | Dynamic ELF | Dynamic / Static | Static PIE | Static ELF |\n| **C Interoperability** | **Direct C ABI** | Native | Native | Via FFI bindings | Direct | Via Cgo (High Overhead) |\n| **Package Manager** | **Built-in (`rad`)** | None | CMake/Conan | Built-in (`cargo`) | Built-in | Built-in (`go`) |\n| **Type Safety** | **Strong, Static** | Weak | Strong | Strict Lifetime Borrowing | Strong | Strong |\n| **Compilation Speed** | **Fast (Direct SSA IR)** | Very Fast | Slow | Slow to Moderate | Fast | Very Fast |\n| **Toolchain Self-Containment** | **Full (Sysroot Included)** | External | External | External Linkers | Self-Contained | Self-Contained |\n\n---\n\n## 1.4 When to Choose Rad\n\nChoose Rad when you need:\n- High-performance systems software (network daemons, CLI utilities, games, operating system tools, database engines).\n- Executables that must run predictably across all Linux distributions without \"missing glibc version\" errors.\n- A clean, modern syntax that eliminates C's header duplication and macro pitfalls while avoiding extreme compiler complexity.\n- Direct, frictionless calls into legacy C codebases and standard Unix APIs.\n",
  "02_installation.md": "# Chapter 2: Installation & Toolchain Setup\n\n## 2.1 Quick Installation (Automated)\n\nThe quickest way to install the complete, production-ready Rad toolchain on Linux is using the official installer script via `curl`:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GamerxIOEPB/rad-lang/main/scripts/install.sh | sh\n```\n\n### What the Installer Does:\n1. Detects your host architecture (`x86_64`) and operating system (`Linux`).\n2. Downloads the official release archive (`rad-0.1.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz`) from GitHub Releases.\n3. Verifies the cryptographic SHA-256 integrity hash against `SHA256SUMS`.\n4. Extracts all binaries, libraries, target sysroots, and LLVM tools to `~/.rad`.\n5. Automatically configures your shell environment (`~/.bashrc`, `~/.zshrc`, or `~/.profile`) to add `~/.rad/bin` to your `PATH`.\n\nTo verify your installation in the current terminal, source your shell profile or restart your terminal:\n\n```bash\nsource ~/.bashrc\nrad --version\nradc --version\n```\n\nExpected output:\n```text\nrad 0.1.0 (x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)\nradc 0.1.0 (self-hosted x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.2 Verifying with `rad doctor`\n\nRad provides an integrated diagnostic doctor command that verifies every component of your installed toolchain:\n\n```bash\nrad doctor\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nRad Toolchain Doctor\n====================\n\n  [OK] radc compiler: found (/home/username/.rad/bin/radc)\n  [OK] LLVM backend (rad-llc): found\n  [OK] Linker (rad-lld): found\n  [OK] Target sysroot (x86_64-unknown-linux-musl): verified at /home/username/.rad/lib/targets/x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\n\nStatus: Toolchain is healthy and ready for self-contained native compilation.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.3 Non-Interactive & Custom Prefix Installation\n\nFor continuous integration (CI) workflows, Docker containers, or system-wide deployments, you can pass arguments to the installer:\n\n```bash\n# Non-interactive install without shell prompts:\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GamerxIOEPB/rad-lang/main/scripts/install.sh | sh -s -- -y\n\n# Install to a custom directory prefix (e.g., /opt/rad):\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GamerxIOEPB/rad-lang/main/scripts/install.sh | sh -s -- --prefix /opt/rad -y\n\n# Install without modifying shell configuration files:\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GamerxIOEPB/rad-lang/main/scripts/install.sh | sh -s -- --no-modify-path -y\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.4 Building from Source\n\nIf you prefer building the toolchain from source repository:\n\n### Prerequisites:\n- Git\n- GNU Make\n- Host Clang & LLVM development libraries\n- Existing `radc` compiler (or reference compiler)\n\n### Build Steps:\n```bash\n# Clone the repository\ngit clone https://github.com/GamerxIOEPB/rad-lang.git\ncd rad-lang\n\n# Build all binaries (compiler, package manager, static PIE launchers)\nmake all\n\n# Verify the freshly built binaries in bin/\n./bin/radc --version\n./bin/rad doctor\n\n# Install into default prefix (~/.rad)\nmake install\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.5 Toolchain Directory Layout\n\nWhen installed into `~/.rad`, the directory structure is organized as follows:\n\n```\n~/.rad/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 bin/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 rad           # Rad package manager & build orchestrator\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 radc          # Self-hosted native Rad compiler\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 rad-llc       # Static PIE launcher for LLVM code generation\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 rad-lld       # Static PIE launcher for LLD GNU linker\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 rad-clang     # Bundled C compiler launcher\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 rad-clang++   # Bundled C++ compiler launcher\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 lib/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 llvm/         # Real LLVM binaries (llc_real, ld.lld_real, clang) and shared libs\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 clang/        # Clang resource headers (stddef.h, stdarg.h, etc.)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 rad/          # Rad standard library sources (core, alloc, std)\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 targets/\n\u2502       \u2514\u2500\u2500 x86_64-unknown-linux-musl/\n\u2502           \u251c\u2500\u2500 crt/  # C runtime startup objects (rcrt1.o, crti.o, crtn.o)\n\u2502           \u2514\u2500\u2500 lib/  # Static libraries (libc.a, libclang_rt.builtins-x86_64.a, libunwind.a)\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 share/\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500 rad/          # License manifests, release metadata, and targets.toml\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.6 Environment Variables\n\nThe toolchain respects the following environment variables:\n\n- **`RAD_HOME`**: Overrides the installation directory prefix (default: `~/.rad`).\n- **`PATH`**: Ensure `$RAD_HOME/bin` is in your `PATH`.\n- **`RADC`**: Explicit path to the `radc` binary used when executing `make` or build scripts.\n",
  "03_getting_started.md": "# Chapter 3: Getting Started\n\n## 3.1 Your First Rad Program (\"Hello, World!\")\n\nLet's start by writing and compiling a standalone Rad program from scratch.\n\n### 1. Create a Source File\nCreate a new file named `hello.rad`:\n\n```rad\n// hello.rad \u2014 My first Rad program\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let message: str = \"Hello, World from Rad!\"\n    let year: i32 = 2026\n\n    println(\"{} - Welcome to {}\", message, year)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Compile with `radc`\nInvoke the self-hosted Rad compiler directly:\n\n```bash\nradc hello.rad -o hello\n```\n\n### 3. Run the Native Executable\n```bash\n./hello\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nHello, World from Rad! - Welcome to 2026\n```\n\n### 4. Inspect the Executable\nCheck the properties of the resulting binary using standard Linux tools:\n\n```bash\nfile hello\nldd hello\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nhello: ELF 64-bit LSB pie executable, x86-64, version 1 (SYSV), statically linked, stripped\nnot a dynamic executable\n```\n\nNotice that `hello` is **statically linked** and **not a dynamic executable**! It has zero dependencies on host glibc versions and will run on any 64-bit Linux machine.\n\n---\n\n## 3.2 Using the Rad Package Manager (`rad`)\n\nFor anything beyond single-file scripts, use the `rad` package manager to initialize, build, and organize your projects.\n\n### Creating a New Application Package\nRun `rad new` followed by the project name:\n\n```bash\nrad new my_project\ncd my_project\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nCreating new Rad package...\nCreated binary (application) package successfully!\n```\n\n### Project Directory Layout\n`rad new` creates a standard directory layout:\n\n```\nmy_project/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 rad.toml        # Package manifest & configuration\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 .gitignore      # Ignores target/ build output and temporary files\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 src/\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500 main.rad    # Main application entry point\n```\n\n### The Manifest (`rad.toml`)\nThe `rad.toml` file defines package metadata:\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"my_project\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2026\"\n\n[dependencies]\n```\n\n### The Generated Code (`src/main.rad`)\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    println(\"Hello from Rad Package!\")\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3.3 Building and Running Packages\n\n### Checking Syntax without Compiling\nTo rapidly check your code for syntax errors and structural validity without performing full LLVM compilation:\n\n```bash\nrad check\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nChecking Rad package syntax and types...\nCheck complete: 0 errors.\nCheck passed: 0 errors.\n```\n\n### Compiling with `rad build`\nCompile the package into a debug executable:\n\n```bash\nrad build\n```\n\nThis compiles `src/main.rad` into `target/debug/main`.\n\nTo build an optimized release binary with LLVM `-O3` optimizations:\n\n```bash\nrad build --release\n```\n\nThis places the optimized binary in `target/release/main`.\n\n### Running with `rad run`\nBuild and immediately execute your application in one step:\n\n```bash\nrad run\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nCompiling project with radc...\nFinished build successfully.\nRunning...\nHello from Rad Package!\n```\n\nYou can pass command-line arguments to your application by separating them after `--`:\n\n```bash\nrad run -- arg1 arg2\n```\n\n### Cleaning Build Artifacts\nTo wipe generated binaries and object files:\n\n```bash\nrad clean\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3.4 Creating a Library Package\n\nIf you are developing a reusable library rather than an executable application, pass the `--lib` flag:\n\n```bash\nrad new my_library --lib\n```\n\nThis generates `src/lib.rad` instead of `src/main.rad`.\n",
  "04_variables_and_types.md": "# Chapter 4: Variables, Mutability & Types\n\n## 4.1 Variables & Mutability\n\nIn Rad, variables are declared using either `let` or `var`.\n\n### Immutable Variables (`let`)\nBy default, variables introduced with `let` are immutable. Once initialized, their value cannot be changed:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let x: i64 = 42\n    // x = 50  <-- Error: assignment to immutable variable\n    println(\"x is: {x}\")\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Mutable Variables (`var`)\nWhen a variable needs to be reassigned or mutated, declare it using `var`:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var count: i64 = 0\n    count = count + 1\n    count = count * 2\n    println(\"count is: {count}\")\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Type Inference\nRad infers the type of a variable from its initializer expression when omitted:\n\n```rad\nlet message = \"Hello from Rad\" // inferred as str\nlet active = true             // inferred as bool\nvar counter = 100i64          // inferred as i64 from suffix\n```\n\nHowever, explicit type annotations (`name: Type`) are recommended for public functions and clear code documentation.\n\n---\n\n## 4.2 Primitive Data Types\n\nRad provides an explicit, machine-level set of scalar data types.\n\n### 1. Integer Types\nRad provides signed and unsigned integers across standard bit widths:\n\n| Type | Signed | Bit Width | Description / Range | Literal Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `i8` | Yes | 8 | -128 to 127 | `10i8` |\n| `i16` | Yes | 16 | -32,768 to 32,767 | `1000i16` |\n| `i32` | Yes | 32 | -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 (default int) | `42`, `42i32` |\n| `i64` | Yes | 64 | -9,223,372,036,854,775,808 to 9,223,372,036,854,775,807 | `100i64` |\n| `isize` | Yes | Pointer width | Target architecture signed pointer size (64-bit on x86_64) | `5isize` |\n| `u8` | No | 8 | 0 to 255 (byte) | `255u8` |\n| `u16` | No | 16 | 0 to 65,535 | `60000u16` |\n| `u32` | No | 32 | 0 to 4,294,967,295 | `100000u32` |\n| `u64` | No | 64 | 0 to 18,446,744,073,709,551,615 | `1000000u64` |\n| `usize` | No | Pointer width | Target architecture unsigned pointer size (64-bit on x86_64) | `0usize` |\n\n### 2. Floating-Point Types\n- `f32`: Single-precision 32-bit IEEE 754 floating point.\n- `f64`: Double-precision 64-bit IEEE 754 floating point.\n\n### 3. Boolean Type (`bool`)\nThe `bool` type represents logical truth values:\n- `true`\n- `false`\n\n```rad\nlet is_running: bool = true\nlet is_finished: bool = false\n```\n\n### 4. String Literals (`str`)\nThe `str` type represents a null-terminated pointer to an immutable sequence of UTF-8 bytes:\n\n```rad\nlet greeting: str = \"Welcome to Rad Systems\"\n```\n\nString literals support standard escape sequences:\n- `\\n`: Newline\n- `\\t`: Tab\n- `\\r`: Carriage return\n- `\\\"`: Double quote\n- `\\\\`: Backslash\n- `\\0`: Null terminator byte\n\n---\n\n## 4.3 Raw Pointer Types\n\nFor low-level systems programming, operating system interactions, and C ABI interop, Rad provides raw pointers:\n\n- `*raw T`: Immutable raw pointer to type `T`.\n- `*raw var T`: Mutable raw pointer to type `T`.\n- `*raw *raw u8`: Multi-level raw pointer (such as `argv: *raw *raw u8`).\n\n```rad\n// Pointer declaration and casting\nlet ptr: *raw u8 = greeting as *raw u8\nlet first_byte: u8 = ptr[0usize]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4.4 Constants (`const`)\n\nConstants are compile-time immutable values evaluated before code generation. They are declared at file scope with the `const` keyword:\n\n```rad\nconst MAX_BUFFER_SIZE: i64 = 4096\nconst DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS: i64 = 5000\nconst PROTOCOL_VERSION: i32 = 1\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    println(\"Max buffer: {}\", MAX_BUFFER_SIZE)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4.5 Comments\n\nRad supports both single-line and multi-line block comments:\n\n```rad\n// This is a single-line comment\n\n/*\n * This is a multi-line\n * block comment.\n */\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let a = 10 // Inline comment\n    return 0\n}\n```\n",
  "05_operators_and_expressions.md": "# Chapter 5: Operators & Expressions\n\n## 5.1 Arithmetic Operators\n\nRad supports the standard set of binary and unary arithmetic operators:\n\n| Operator | Name | Example | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `+` | Addition | `a + b` | Calculates the sum of `a` and `b` |\n| `-` | Subtraction | `a - b` | Calculates the difference of `b` from `a` |\n| `*` | Multiplication | `a * b` | Calculates the product of `a` and `b` |\n| `/` | Division | `a / b` | Integer or floating-point quotient |\n| `%` | Modulo | `a % b` | Remainder of integer division |\n| `-` | Negation (unary) | `-x` | Negates the numeric operand |\n\n### Example:\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let a: i64 = 25\n    let b: i64 = 4\n\n    let sum = a + b       // 29\n    let diff = a - b      // 21\n    let prod = a * b      // 100\n    let quot = a / b      // 6\n    let rem = a % b       // 1\n    let neg = -a          // -25\n\n    println(\"sum = {}, diff = {}, prod = {}, quot = {}, rem = {}\", sum, diff, prod, quot, rem)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5.2 Comparison Operators\n\nComparison operators evaluate expressions and return a `bool` (`true` or `false`):\n\n| Operator | Description | Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `==` | Equal to | `a == b` |\n| `!=` | Not equal to | `a != b` |\n| `<` | Less than | `a < b` |\n| `<=` | Less than or equal to | `a <= b` |\n| `>` | Greater than | `a > b` |\n| `>=` | Greater than or equal to | `a >= b` |\n\n### Example:\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let score: i64 = 85\n\n    let is_passing: bool = score >= 50\n    let is_perfect: bool = score == 100\n\n    if is_passing && !is_perfect {\n        println(\"Passed with score: {score}\")\n    }\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5.3 Logical Operators\n\nRad provides standard short-circuiting Boolean logic operators:\n\n| Operator | Name | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `&&` | Logical AND | Returns `true` only if both operands are `true` (short-circuits) |\n| `\\|\\|` | Logical OR | Returns `true` if either operand is `true` (short-circuits) |\n| `!` | Logical NOT | Inverts the boolean operand |\n\n### Example:\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let has_key = true\n    let is_admin = false\n    let is_emergency = true\n\n    if (has_key || is_admin) && !is_emergency {\n        println(\"Standard access granted\")\n    } else if is_emergency {\n        println(\"Emergency override active!\")\n    }\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5.4 Operator Precedence\n\nOperators in Rad are evaluated following standard mathematical and systems programming precedence rules (from highest to lowest binding power):\n\n1. **Postfix**: Member access (`.`), Method call (`.method()`), Function call (`()`), Indexing (`[]`)\n2. **Unary**: Negation (`-`), Logical NOT (`!`)\n3. **Multiplicative**: `*`, `/`, `%`\n4. **Additive**: `+`, `-`\n5. **Relational / Comparison**: `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`\n6. **Equality**: `==`, `!=`\n7. **Logical AND**: `&&`\n8. **Logical OR**: `||`\n9. **Cast**: `as`\n10. **Assignment**: `=`\n\nUse parentheses `( ... )` to explicitly enforce evaluation order when combining complex expressions.\n\n---\n\n## 5.5 Explicit Type Casting (`as`)\n\nRad does not perform silent lossy implicit conversions between incompatible types. Conversions between numeric types, pointers, and memory representations require an explicit `as` cast:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let big: i64 = 1000\n    let small: i32 = big as i32\n\n    let val: u64 = 0xDEADBEEF\n    let as_ptr: *raw u8 = val as *raw u8\n    let back_to_num: usize = as_ptr as usize\n\n    println(\"small = {}, ptr_addr = {}\", small, back_to_num)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Common Cast Patterns:\n- Converting numeric width: `val_i64 as i32`, `byte as i64`\n- Casting pointers to integer addresses: `ptr as usize`\n- Casting integer addresses to raw pointers: `addr as *raw u8`\n- Casting byte pointers to string slices: `byte_ptr as str`\n",
  "06_control_flow.md": "# Chapter 6: Control Flow\n\n## 6.1 Conditional Branching (`if`, `else if`, `else`)\n\nRad uses `if` expressions to execute blocks of code based on a Boolean condition:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let temperature: i64 = 28\n\n    if temperature > 35 {\n        println(\"Heat warning!\")\n    } else if temperature > 20 {\n        println(\"Pleasant warm weather.\")\n    } else if temperature > 10 {\n        println(\"Cool weather.\")\n    } else {\n        println(\"Cold weather alert!\")\n    }\n\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Key Rules:\n- The condition expression must evaluate to a `bool`.\n- Parentheses around the condition are optional (`if x > 0` vs `if (x > 0)`).\n- The curly braces `{ ... }` around the then-block and else-block are **mandatory**, eliminating classic C \"dangling else\" vulnerabilities.\n\n---\n\n## 6.2 While Loops (`while`)\n\nRad provides the `while` loop for repeated execution while a Boolean condition remains `true`:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var counter: i64 = 0\n\n    while counter < 5 {\n        println(\"Iteration: {counter}\")\n        counter = counter + 1\n    }\n\n    println(\"Loop completed.\")\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nIteration: 0\nIteration: 1\nIteration: 2\nIteration: 3\nIteration: 4\nLoop completed.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6.3 Loop Control (`break` & `continue`)\n\n### The `break` Statement\n`break` immediately exits the enclosing loop:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var i: i64 = 0\n\n    while true {\n        if i >= 4 {\n            break // Exits the loop when i reaches 4\n        }\n        println(\"i = {i}\")\n        i = i + 1\n    }\n\n    println(\"Broke out of loop.\")\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### The `continue` Statement\n`continue` skips the remainder of the current loop iteration and proceeds immediately to the next condition evaluation:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var i: i64 = 0\n\n    while i < 10 {\n        i = i + 1\n        if i % 2 == 0 {\n            continue // Skip even numbers\n        }\n        println(\"Odd number: {i}\")\n    }\n\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nOdd number: 1\nOdd number: 3\nOdd number: 5\nOdd number: 7\nOdd number: 9\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6.4 Infinite Loops\n\nYou can write infinite service loops or polling loops using `while true`:\n\n```rad\nfn poll_events() {\n    var running: bool = true\n    while running {\n        // Poll event queue\n        let should_exit = check_exit_signal()\n        if should_exit {\n            running = false\n            break\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n",
  "07_functions.md": "# Chapter 7: Functions & Program Entry\n\n## 7.1 Declaring Functions\n\nFunctions in Rad are declared using the `fn` keyword, followed by the function name, a comma-separated parameter list enclosed in parentheses, an optional return type arrow (`-> Type`), and a mandatory body block enclosed in `{ ... }`:\n\n```rad\nfn add(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 {\n    return a + b\n}\n\nfn greet(name: str) {\n    println(\"Hello, {}!\", name)\n}\n```\n\nIf a function does not return a value, the `-> Type` annotation can be omitted (implicitly returning `void`).\n\n---\n\n## 7.2 The `return` Statement\n\nIn Rad, functions that return a value must explicitly use the `return` statement:\n\n```rad\nfn max_val(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 {\n    if a > b {\n        return a\n    } else {\n        return b\n    }\n}\n```\n\nFunctions can also use early returns for guard clauses:\n\n```rad\nfn compute_ratio(numerator: i64, denominator: i64) -> i64 {\n    if denominator == 0 {\n        println(\"Error: division by zero!\")\n        return 0\n    }\n    return numerator / denominator\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7.3 Mutable Parameters (`var`)\n\nFunction arguments are immutable by default within the function body. If a parameter's local copy needs to be mutated, prefix the parameter name with `var`:\n\n```rad\nfn countdown(var count: i64) {\n    while count > 0 {\n        println(\"T-minus {count}...\")\n        count = count - 1\n    }\n    println(\"Liftoff!\")\n}\n```\n\nNotice that parameters in Rad are passed by value: modifying `var count` modifies the local parameter variable and does not affect the caller's variable.\n\n---\n\n## 7.4 Public Visibility (`pub`)\n\nWhen authoring multi-file projects or reusable libraries, items that should be accessible outside their defining module are marked with `pub`:\n\n```rad\npub fn calculate_hash(data: *raw u8, len: usize) -> u64 {\n    // Implementation\n    return 0u64\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7.5 The `main` Entry Point\n\nEvery executable Rad program begins execution at the `main` function. Rad supports two valid signatures for `main`:\n\n### 1. Simple Zero-Argument Entry:\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    println(\"Application running\")\n    return 0 // Process exit code 0 (success)\n}\n```\n\nOr omitting the explicit return type:\n```rad\nfn main() {\n    println(\"Exits with code 0 by default\")\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Command-Line Arguments Entry:\nTo receive command-line arguments passed from the operating system shell:\n\n```rad\nfn main(argc: i32, argv: *raw *raw u8) -> i32 {\n    println(\"Program name: {}\", argv[0usize] as str)\n    println(\"Argument count: {}\", argc)\n\n    var i: usize = 1usize\n    while i < (argc as usize) {\n        let arg_str: str = argv[i] as str\n        println(\"Arg #{}: {}\", i, arg_str)\n        i = i + 1usize\n    }\n\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nHere:\n- `argc`: Number of arguments (including program name at index 0).\n- `argv`: Raw pointer to an array of null-terminated C string pointers (`*raw *raw u8`).\n- Returning an integer value sets the Linux process exit status (returned to the calling shell via `$?`).\n",
  "08_structs_and_methods.md": "# Chapter 8: Structs, Fields & Methods\n\n## 8.1 Defining Structs\n\nStructs in Rad allow you to group related fields into a custom compound data type:\n\n```rad\nstruct Point {\n    x: i64,\n    y: i64,\n}\n\nstruct Player {\n    id: i64,\n    health: i32,\n    score: i64,\n}\n```\n\nEach field declaration consists of `field_name: Type`, separated by commas.\n\n---\n\n## 8.2 Instantiation & Field Initialization\n\nCreate an instance of a struct using struct literal syntax (`StructName { field: value, ... }`):\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let p = Point {\n        x: 10,\n        y: 20,\n    }\n\n    println(\"Point: x={}, y={}\", p.x, p.y)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8.3 Accessing and Mutating Fields\n\n### Accessing Fields\nAccess struct fields using the dot operator (`.`):\n\n```rad\nlet px = p.x\nlet py = p.y\n```\n\n### Mutating Fields\nIf a struct variable is declared with `var`, its fields can be reassigned:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var p = Point { x: 5, y: 12 }\n    println(\"Initial: x={}, y={}\", p.x, p.y)\n\n    p.x = 100\n    p.y = 200\n    println(\"Updated: x={}, y={}\", p.x, p.y)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8.4 Methods on Structs (`self` Receiver)\n\nIn Rad, methods are functions that take an explicit `self` pointer receiver as their first parameter:\n\n```rad\nstruct Rectangle {\n    width: i64,\n    height: i64,\n}\n\n// Method calculating the area of a Rectangle\nfn area(self: *raw Rectangle) -> i64 {\n    return self.width * self.height\n}\n\n// Method calculating the perimeter of a Rectangle\nfn perimeter(self: *raw Rectangle) -> i64 {\n    return 2 * (self.width + self.height)\n}\n```\n\n### Invoking Methods with Dot Syntax\nOnce a function is defined with a `(self: *raw StructName)` receiver, it can be called directly on an instance using dot notation:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let rect = Rectangle { width: 10, height: 5 }\n\n    // Calling methods via dot notation:\n    let a = rect.area()\n    let p = rect.perimeter()\n\n    println(\"Area: {}, Perimeter: {}\", a, p)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nArea: 50, Perimeter: 30\n```\n\n### Methods That Mutate Fields\nTo mutate the struct inside a method, declare `self` as a mutable raw pointer (`self: *raw var StructName`):\n\n```rad\nfn scale(self: *raw var Rectangle, factor: i64) {\n    self.width = self.width * factor\n    self.height = self.height * factor\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var rect = Rectangle { width: 4, height: 6 }\n    rect.scale(3)\n    println(\"Scaled rectangle: width={}, height={}\", rect.width, rect.height)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8.5 Constructor Functions\n\nBy convention, constructor helper functions are written as factory functions returning a new struct instance:\n\n```rad\nfn make_point(x: i64, y: i64) -> Point {\n    return Point { x: x, y: y }\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let pt = make_point(30, 40)\n    println(\"pt = ({}, {})\", pt.x, pt.y)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Do not name user functions simply `new()`, as `@new` is reserved internally by `radc`'s code generator for built-in heap allocations of strings and dynamic vectors. Use descriptive names like `make_point()`, `Point_create()`, or `init()`.\n",
  "09_pointers_and_memory.md": "# Chapter 9: Pointers, Memory & Casting\n\n## 9.1 Raw Pointers (`*raw T`)\n\nRad provides low-level raw pointers that represent direct hardware memory addresses:\n\n- `*raw T`: An immutable pointer to memory holding values of type `T`.\n- `*raw var T`: A mutable pointer through which the underlying memory can be modified.\n- `*raw u8`: A raw byte pointer (analogous to `const char*` or `const uint8_t*` in C).\n- `*raw var u8`: A mutable raw byte pointer (analogous to `char*` or `uint8_t*` in C).\n\n---\n\n## 9.2 Pointer Indexing & Dereferencing\n\nPointers in Rad can be indexed directly using bracket syntax (`ptr[index]`), functioning like an unbounded array view:\n\n```rad\nfn print_bytes(ptr: *raw u8, len: usize) {\n    var i: usize = 0usize\n    while i < len {\n        let b: u8 = ptr[i] // Read byte at offset i\n        println(\"Byte #{}: {}\", i, b)\n        i = i + 1usize\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Mutating Through Pointer Indexing\n```rad\nfn zero_buffer(buf: *raw var u8, len: usize) {\n    var i: usize = 0usize\n    while i < len {\n        buf[i] = 0u8 // Write byte at offset i\n        i = i + 1usize\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9.3 Dynamic Memory Allocation (`malloc`, `realloc`, `free`)\n\nBecause Rad programs link against the C standard library (`libc.a`), the standard heap allocation primitives are built directly into the compiler:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    // 1. Allocate 64 bytes on the heap\n    let size: usize = 64usize\n    let buffer: *raw var u8 = malloc(size) as *raw var u8\n\n    if (buffer as usize) == 0usize {\n        println(\"Memory allocation failed!\")\n        return 1\n    }\n\n    // 2. Write data into the allocated buffer\n    var i: usize = 0usize\n    while i < 10usize {\n        buffer[i] = (65usize + i) as u8 // Write ASCII 'A', 'B', 'C', ...\n        i = i + 1usize\n    }\n    buffer[10usize] = 0u8 // Null-terminate\n\n    // 3. Print buffer as a string slice\n    println(\"Buffer string: {}\", buffer as str)\n\n    // 4. Free allocated memory\n    free(buffer as *raw u8)\n    println(\"Memory freed successfully.\")\n\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nBuffer string: ABCDEFGHIJ\nMemory freed successfully.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9.4 Pointer Arithmetic & Offsets\n\nPointer arithmetic in Rad is performed transparently using index offsets (`ptr[offset]`) or by converting the pointer to an address integer with `as usize`, performing byte-level arithmetic, and casting back:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let msg: str = \"Rad Systems Programming\"\n    let p_msg: *raw u8 = msg as *raw u8\n\n    // Skip the first 4 bytes (\"Rad \") by pointer arithmetic:\n    let addr: usize = (p_msg as usize) + 4usize\n    let p_sub: *raw u8 = addr as *raw u8\n\n    println(\"Original: {}\", msg)\n    println(\"Sub-slice: {}\", p_sub as str)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nOriginal: Rad Systems Programming\nSub-slice: Systems Programming\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9.5 Memory Safety Best Practices\n\n1. **Always Match `malloc` with `free`**: For heap allocations created with `malloc()`, ensure they are freed with `free()` when no longer needed to prevent memory leaks.\n2. **Prefer Built-In `String` and `Vec`**: In application code, prefer the standard heap collections `String` and `Vec`, which manage their own buffers, reallocation, and growth automatically.\n3. **Check for Null Pointers**: Validate pointer addresses against zero (`(ptr as usize) == 0usize`) when receiving pointers from OS APIs or C libraries.\n",
  "10_strings_and_collections.md": "# Chapter 10: Strings, Collections & Formatting\n\n## 10.1 The Built-In `String` Type\n\nRad provides a heap-allocated, dynamically resizable `String` type built directly into the compiler's code generator.\n\n### Creating Strings\n- **`String.new()`**: Creates a new empty dynamic string with initial preallocated heap capacity.\n- **`String.from_str(s: str)`**: Creates a dynamic string initialized with a copy of an existing string literal or slice.\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var s1 = String.new()\n    var s2 = String.from_str(\"Hello, Rad!\")\n\n    println(\"s2 = {}\", s2.as_ptr())\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Mutating Strings\n- **`s.push_str(src: str)`**: Appends a null-terminated string slice to the end of `s`. Automatically reallocates buffer capacity if needed.\n- **`s.push_byte(byte: u8)`**: Appends a single byte (character) to `s`.\n- **`s.len()`**: Returns the current byte length of the string (excluding the null terminator).\n- **`s.as_ptr()`**: Returns a null-terminated raw byte pointer (`*raw u8` / `str`) to the internal buffer, suitable for printing or passing to C APIs.\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var greeting = String.from_str(\"Welcome\")\n    greeting.push_str(\" to the Rad Language\")\n    greeting.push_byte(33u8) // '!'\n\n    println(\"Text: {}\", greeting.as_ptr())\n    println(\"Byte count: {}\", greeting.len())\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nText: Welcome to the Rad Language!\nByte count: 28\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10.2 The Built-In `Vec` Type\n\nRad provides a dynamically resizable heap vector (`Vec`) for sequential collections:\n\n### Creating Vectors\n- **`Vec.new()`**: Allocates a new vector with initial capacity.\n\n### Vector Operations\n- **`v.push(item: i64)`**: Appends an element to the end of the vector, doubling capacity when full.\n- **`v.pop() -> i64`**: Removes and returns the last element from the vector (returns `0` if empty).\n- **`v.len`**: Struct field containing the current number of elements in the vector.\n- **`v.cap`**: Struct field containing the allocated capacity.\n- **`v.ptr`**: Raw pointer to the element array (`*raw i64` or `*raw u8`).\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var numbers = Vec.new()\n\n    // Append items\n    numbers.push(10)\n    numbers.push(20)\n    numbers.push(30)\n\n    println(\"Vector length: {}\", numbers.len)\n\n    // Access elements via raw pointer:\n    let p: *raw i64 = numbers.ptr as *raw i64\n    println(\"numbers[0] = {}, numbers[1] = {}\", p[0usize], p[1usize])\n\n    // Pop the last element:\n    let last = numbers.pop()\n    println(\"Popped item: {}, remaining count: {}\", last, numbers.len)\n\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nVector length: 3\nnumbers[0] = 10, numbers[1] = 20\nPopped item: 30, remaining count: 2\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10.3 Formatted Printing with `println()`\n\nRad's `println()` function provides two complementary formatting modes:\n\n### 1. Positional `{}` Placeholders\nPass a format template string followed by the arguments to print:\n\n```rad\nlet name: str = \"Alice\"\nlet age: i32 = 30\nlet city: str = \"Zurich\"\n\nprintln(\"Name: {}, Age: {}, City: {}\", name, age, city)\n```\n\n### 2. Variable Interpolation (`{var_name}`)\nWhen passing a single template string, Rad automatically expands `{variable}` patterns:\n\n```rad\nlet width: i64 = 1920\nlet height: i64 = 1080\n\nprintln(\"Resolution: {width} x {height}\")\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nResolution: 1920 x 1080\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10.4 Standard C Formatting (`printf`)\n\nFor complex C-style format specifiers (`%08x`, `%.2f`, `%p`), you can call the standard libc `printf` function directly:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let hex_val: i64 = 255\n    printf(\"Hexadecimal: 0x%04X\\n\", hex_val)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n",
  "11_c_interop.md": "# Chapter 11: C & C++ Interoperability\n\n## 11.1 The Rad C ABI Philosophy\n\nRad does not treat C as an afterthought or an awkward foreign interface. Rad is designed with **mechanical sympathy** for the C ABI:\n\n- Primitive integers (`i32`, `i64`, `u8`, `usize`) directly map to C hardware types (`int32_t`, `int64_t`, `uint8_t`, `size_t`).\n- Pointers (`*raw u8`, `*raw var T`) use standard 64-bit machine pointer representation.\n- Functions adhere strictly to the System V AMD64 ABI calling convention on Linux.\n- Executables statically link against Musl libc (`libc.a`).\n\nAs a result, calling C functions from Rad\u2014or calling Rad functions from C\u2014incurs **zero runtime conversion overhead, zero boxing, and zero marshaling layers**.\n\n---\n\n## 11.2 Built-In C Standard Library Functions\n\nThe Rad compiler's code generator automatically declares and links the most common POSIX / C standard library routines into every generated binary:\n\n| Function | Signature in Rad / LLVM | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `puts(s: str) -> i32` | `declare i32 @puts(ptr)` | Prints string followed by newline |\n| `printf(fmt: str, ...) -> i32` | `declare i32 @printf(ptr, ...)` | Formatted C printing |\n| `system(cmd: str) -> i32` | `declare i32 @system(ptr)` | Executes a shell command via `sh` |\n| `malloc(bytes: usize) -> *raw var u8` | `declare ptr @malloc(i64)` | Allocates heap memory |\n| `realloc(p: *raw u8, bytes: usize) -> *raw var u8` | `declare ptr @realloc(ptr, i64)` | Resizes allocated heap memory |\n| `free(p: *raw u8)` | `declare void @free(ptr)` | Deallocates heap memory |\n| `fopen(path: str, mode: str) -> *raw u8` | `declare ptr @fopen(ptr, ptr)` | Opens a file stream |\n| `fclose(fp: *raw u8) -> i32` | `declare i32 @fclose(ptr)` | Closes a file stream |\n| `fread(buf: *raw var u8, sz: i64, n: i64, fp: *raw u8) -> i64` | `declare i64 @fread(ptr, i64, i64, ptr)` | Reads binary data from stream |\n| `fwrite(buf: *raw u8, sz: i64, n: i64, fp: *raw u8) -> i64` | `declare i64 @fwrite(ptr, i64, i64, ptr)` | Writes binary data to stream |\n| `fseek(fp: *raw u8, off: i64, whence: i32) -> i32` | `declare i32 @fseek(ptr, i64, i32)` | Seeks stream position |\n| `ftell(fp: *raw u8) -> i64` | `declare i64 @ftell(ptr)` | Returns stream offset |\n| `getenv(name: str) -> str` | `declare ptr @getenv(ptr)` | Reads environment variable |\n\n### Example: Reading an Environment Variable and Running a Subprocess\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let user = getenv(\"USER\")\n    if (user as usize) != 0usize {\n        println(\"Current Linux user: {}\", user)\n    }\n\n    // Call libc system() directly:\n    system(\"uname -a\")\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11.3 Compiling & Linking C Code with Rad\n\nWhen your project includes custom C algorithms, hardware device drivers, or third-party C libraries:\n\n### Step 1: Write Your C Code\nCreate `native_math.c`:\n```c\n// native_math.c\n#include <stdint.h>\n\nint64_t native_fast_add(int64_t a, int64_t b) {\n    return (a * 2) + (b * 2);\n}\n```\n\n### Step 2: Compile C Object with `rad-clang`\nUse Rad's bundled C compiler:\n```bash\nrad-clang -c native_math.c -o native_math.o\n```\n\n### Step 3: Call and Link in Rad\nCreate `main.rad`:\n```rad\n// main.rad\nfn native_fast_add(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 {\n    // Linked symbol from native_math.o\n    return 0\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let res = native_fast_add(10, 20)\n    println(\"Native math result: {res}\")\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nCompile and link everything in one command:\n```bash\nradc main.rad native_math.o -o my_app\n./my_app\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11.4 Compiling C++ with `rad-clang++`\n\nRad bundles `rad-clang++` alongside `rad-clang`, complete with bundled C++ standard library headers.\n\nTo compile C++ translation units:\n```bash\nrad-clang++ -c helper.cpp -o helper.o\n```\n\nWhen linking C++ objects that use the standard C++ library (`std::string`, `std::vector`, `std::cout`), link with `-lstdc++` or `-lc++`:\n```bash\nradc main.rad helper.o --linker \"rad-clang++ -static\" -o my_cpp_app\n```\n",
  "12_compiler_cli.md": "# Chapter 12: Compiler (`radc`) & Build Deep-Dive\n\n## 12.1 Compiler CLI Flags (`radc`)\n\nThe `radc` binary is the self-hosted Rad compiler. Its command-line interface provides precise control over code generation, optimization, and linking:\n\n```bash\nradc <file1.rad> [file2.rad ...] [options]\n```\n\n### Options Reference:\n\n| Flag | Description | Default |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `-o <path>` | Set output binary path or object path | Auto-named / `a.out` |\n| `-O0` | Disable LLVM optimizations for fastest compile speed | \u2014 |\n| `-O1` | Basic optimizations | \u2014 |\n| `-O2` | Standard production optimization (recommended) | **Default** |\n| `-O3` | Aggressive optimization (vectorization, loop unrolling) | \u2014 |\n| `--emit-llvm`, `--emit-ir` | Emit textual LLVM IR (`.tmp.ll`) without assembling or linking | Disabled |\n| `--check` | Lex and parse all inputs to verify syntax and types without code generation | Disabled |\n| `--target <triple>` | Set compilation target architecture | `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` |\n| `--linker <cmd>` | Override the default linker cascade with a custom command | Bundled `rad-lld` |\n| `-V`, `--version` | Display compiler version and build information | \u2014 |\n\n---\n\n## 12.2 Inspecting Generated LLVM IR (`--emit-llvm`)\n\nBecause `radc` directly emits textual LLVM IR, you can inspect the exact low-level SSA representation produced by the compiler:\n\n```bash\nradc main.rad --emit-llvm -o main.ll\n```\n\nOpen `main.ll` to see the generated LLVM IR:\n\n```llvm\n; ModuleID = 'radc_compiled'\nsource_filename = \"rad\"\ntarget datalayout = \"e-m:e-p270:32:32-p271:32:32-p272:64:64-i64:64-f80:128-n8:16:32:64-S128\"\ntarget triple = \"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu\"\n\ndeclare ptr @malloc(i64)\ndeclare void @free(ptr)\ndeclare i32 @puts(ptr)\n...\n```\n\nThis is invaluable for systems engineers wanting to verify zero-overhead abstractions, loop vectorization, and memory layouts.\n\n---\n\n## 12.3 The Static PIE Linker Cascade\n\nWhen linking an executable, `radc` executes a two-stage cascade:\n\n1. **Object Assembly**:\n   ```bash\n   rad-llc -filetype=obj -relocation-model=pic -O2 input.tmp.ll -o output.o\n   ```\n2. **Static PIE Linking**:\n   ```bash\n   rad-lld -flavor gnu -m elf_x86_64 -static -pie --no-dynamic-linker -z text -z noexecstack \\\n       -o output \\\n       $SYSROOT/crt/rcrt1.o \\\n       $SYSROOT/crt/crti.o \\\n       output.o \\\n       -L$SYSROOT/lib \\\n       --start-group $SYSROOT/lib/libc.a $SYSROOT/lib/libclang_rt.builtins-x86_64.a --end-group \\\n       $SYSROOT/crt/crtn.o\n   ```\n\n### Why Position-Independent Executables (PIE)?\n- **Full ASLR (Address Space Layout Randomization)**: The Linux kernel loads the code, stack, and heap at randomized memory addresses for defense against buffer overflows and ROP (Return-Oriented Programming) attacks.\n- **Static Linking**: Musl's `rcrt1.o` initializes the static executable's own relocations at runtime without requiring an external dynamic linker (`/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2`).\n",
  "13_package_manager.md": "# Chapter 13: Package Manager (`rad`) CLI Reference\n\nThe `rad` executable is the official package manager, project orchestrator, and developer toolchain utility for the Rad language.\n\n## 13.1 Commands Overview\n\n```text\nUsage: rad <command> [options]\n\nCommands:\n  new <name>   Create a new Rad package: `rad new <name> [--lib]`\n  init         Initialize a new Rad package in the current directory\n  build        Compile the current package\n  run          Build and execute the current package\n  test         Run tests for the package\n  check        Analyze package for errors without compiling\n  clean        Remove target build directory\n  doctor       Inspect and verify bundled toolchain and target sysroot\n```\n\n---\n\n## 13.2 Command Details\n\n### 1. `rad new <name>`\nCreates a new directory with a complete Rad project skeleton:\n\n```bash\n# Create an application project (src/main.rad)\nrad new my_app\n\n# Create a library project (src/lib.rad)\nrad new my_lib --lib\n```\n\n### 2. `rad init`\nInitializes a new Rad package in the current working directory without creating a new parent directory:\n\n```bash\nmkdir my_project && cd my_project\nrad init\n```\n\n### 3. `rad check`\nChecks all source files in the project for lexical, syntactic, and structural errors using `radc --check`. Does not invoke code generation or linking, making it instant:\n\n```bash\nrad check\n```\n\n### 4. `rad build`\nCompiles the project into an executable or library artifact:\n\n```bash\n# Debug build (LLVM -O0, places output in target/debug/main)\nrad build\n\n# Production release build (LLVM -O3, places output in target/release/main)\nrad build --release\n\n# Cross-compilation target override:\nrad build --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\n```\n\n### 5. `rad run`\nBuilds and executes the project's primary binary in one step:\n\n```bash\nrad run\n\n# Run with arguments:\nrad run -- --port 8080 --verbose\n```\n\n### 6. `rad clean`\nRemoves the `target/` directory containing intermediate objects, bitcode, and final binaries:\n\n```bash\nrad clean\n```\n\n### 7. `rad doctor`\nVerifies all components of the Rad toolchain, ensuring that `radc`, `rad-llc`, `rad-lld`, and the Musl target sysroot are present and correctly configured:\n\n```bash\nrad doctor\n```\n\n---\n\n## 13.3 Manifest Specification (`rad.toml`)\n\nEvery Rad package root contains a `rad.toml` manifest file:\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"my_app\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2026\"\n\n[dependencies]\n# Dependencies declaration\n```\n",
  "14_standard_library.md": "# Chapter 14: Standard Library Reference\n\nThe Rad Standard Library is organized into three distinct tiers based on dependency boundaries:\n\n```\nlibrary/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 core/   # Foundation math, panic, memory, and fundamental traits\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 alloc/  # Dynamic memory allocation, strings, and collections\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 std/    # System I/O, file system, process execution, time, and networking\n```\n\n---\n\n## 14.1 Core Library (`library/core`)\n\nThe `core` library contains fundamental language operations that do not require dynamic heap allocation.\n\n### Math Operations (`library/core/src/math.rad`)\n```rad\npub fn min(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64\npub fn max(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64\npub fn abs(x: i64) -> i64\npub fn clamp(val: i64, min_val: i64, max_val: i64) -> i64\n```\n\n### Option Type (`library/core/src/option.rad`)\nRepresents optional values (present or absent):\n```rad\npub struct Option {\n    is_some: bool,\n    value: i64,\n}\n\npub fn some(val: i64) -> Option\npub fn none() -> Option\n```\n\n### Result Type (`library/core/src/result.rad`)\nRepresents either success or error:\n```rad\npub struct Result {\n    is_ok: bool,\n    val: i64,\n    err: i64,\n}\n\npub fn ok(val: i64) -> Result\npub fn err(err_code: i64) -> Result\n```\n\n---\n\n## 14.2 Alloc Library (`library/alloc`)\n\nThe `alloc` library provides dynamically sized types backed by heap allocation:\n\n- **`string.rad`**: Heap-allocated UTF-8 string buffer utilities.\n- **`vec.rad`**: Dynamic contiguous array buffer implementation.\n\n---\n\n## 14.3 Standard Library (`library/std`)\n\nThe `std` library exposes operating system functionality:\n\n- **`io.rad`**: Standard streams (`stdin`, `stdout`, `stderr`), line reading, and buffered terminal output.\n- **`fs.rad`**: File system operations (`read_file`, `write_file`, opening file descriptors).\n- **`process.rad`**: Process spawning, exit status queries, and command execution.\n- **`time.rad`**: System monotonic timestamps and duration sleeps.\n- **`thread.rad`**: POSIX thread synchronization and threading primitives.\n",
  "15_editor_support.md": "# Chapter 15: Tooling & Editor Support\n\n## 15.1 Visual Studio Code Extension\n\nRad provides a dedicated VS Code extension located in [`editors/vscode-rad`](file:///home/abhinav/Projects/rad/editors/vscode-rad):\n\n### Features:\n- **Syntax Highlighting**: Complete TextMate grammar covering keywords (`fn`, `let`, `var`, `struct`, `if`, `else`, `while`, `return`, `as`, `pub`, `break`, `continue`), primitive types, operators, numbers, and strings.\n- **Bracket Matching & Auto-Closing**: Automatic bracket, parenthesis, and quote pairing.\n- **Comment Toggling**: Single-line (`//`) and block (`/* */`) comment shortcuts.\n- **Code Snippets**: Quick templates for functions, structs, while loops, and `main()`.\n\n### Installation in VS Code:\nInstall the packaged `.vsix` file using the `code` CLI:\n\n```bash\ncode --install-extension editors/vscode-rad/rad-lang-0.1.0.vsix\n```\n\n---\n\n## 15.2 Tree-Sitter Grammar\n\nFor editors supporting Tree-Sitter (Neovim, Emacs, Helix, Zed), Rad provides an official Tree-Sitter parser grammar in [`editors/tree-sitter-rad`](file:///home/abhinav/Projects/rad/editors/tree-sitter-rad):\n\n- `grammar.js`: Precise formal syntax specification.\n- `queries/highlights.scm`: Syntax tree highlighting queries.\n- `queries/indents.scm`: Automatic indentation logic.\n- `queries/locals.scm`: Scope and local identifier definition queries.\n"
};
